---
description: Git commit approval policy — never auto-commit
alwaysApply: true
---

# Commits require explicit approval

Never create a git commit automatically. The user approves all commits.

- Do NOT run `git commit` (or `git commit --amend`) unless the user explicitly asks for a commit in the current request.
- Completing a task, passing tests, or finishing a build is NOT permission to commit. Stop and let the user decide.
- Staging is fine to prepare, but do not commit on your own initiative.
- When work is ready, summarise what changed and ask whether to commit, rather than committing.
- Pushing (`git push`) and PR creation likewise require an explicit request.
